  "signWarnShort": "64-bit Windows refuses test-signed kernel drivers by default. Without test-signing mode the driver will not load (the app itself is unaffected).",
  "signGuideTitle": "Driver signing setup",
  "signGuideBody": "AuraVAIO uses a self-generated test certificate, not a Microsoft Attestation/EV signature.\n\n[Affected systems] Every 64-bit Windows (Win7 / 8.1 / 10 / 11) refuses test-signed kernel drivers by default; only 32-bit editions generally allow them. Windows 11 merely adds Secure Boot and Memory Integrity hurdles - it is NOT true that Windows 10 or older is unaffected.\n\n[Option A - persistent, recommended]\n1. Open an elevated Command Prompt and run: bcdedit /set testsigning on\n2. Reboot. A 'Test Mode' watermark at the lower right is expected.\n3. If you get 'protected by Secure Boot policy', disable Secure Boot in BIOS (suspend BitLocker first).\n4. On Windows 11, if it still fails: Windows Security > Device security > Core isolation > turn off Memory Integrity, then reboot.\n5. Run as administrator: <install dir>\\driver\\install_vaio.ps1 -Action install\nTo revert: bcdedit /set testsigning off and reboot.\n\n[Option B - one boot only]\nSettings > System > Recovery > Advanced startup > Restart now > Troubleshoot > Advanced options > Startup Settings > Restart > press 7 or F7 'Disable driver signature enforcement'. This resets on the next reboot and is only for a quick check.\n\nAuraASIO is a user-mode COM component and needs none of the above. Full details: <install dir>\\driver\\README.txt.",
